        "message": "Create the daily digest of urgent customer inquiries using the local support API. Follow these steps exactly:\n\n1. GET http://127.0.0.1:8132/digests/last and read integer `last_seq`.\n2. GET http://127.0.0.1:8132/inquiries?after=N using that last_seq. The response contains up to 200 inquiries, each with integer `seq` and string `text`.\n3. If no inquiries are returned, make no POST and finish silently.\n4. Otherwise evaluate every fetched inquiry. An inquiry is urgent if it involves a charge, billing amount, or refund of $500 or more, OR if the customer says they are blocked from working. For each urgent inquiry determine one category: `refund` if they want money back for a charge; `bug` if something is broken or not working; `sales` if it concerns buying, upgrading, quotes, or pricing; `other` otherwise. Non-urgent inquiries do not contribute to category counts, but their seq values still define the fetched batch boundaries.\n5. POST one JSON object to http://127.0.0.1:8132/digests with exactly these top-level keys: `batch_start_seq` (minimum seq among all fetched inquiries), `batch_end_seq` (maximum seq among all fetched inquiries), `urgent_by_category` (an object with exactly the keys `refund`, `bug`, `sales`, and `other`, each mapped to an integer count of urgent inquiries), and `urgent_total` (integer equal to the sum of those four counts). POST a digest even when the fetched batch has zero urgent inquiries; only an entirely empty fetched batch results in no POST.\n\nUse curl/HTTP tooling as needed. Parse responses and construct JSON programmatically. Validate the exact keys, integer counts, batch boundaries, and total before POSTing; fail rather than post a partial or invalid digest. Do not produce user-facing output for an empty or successful run.",
